    "lawCode": "Amd §196-a, Lab L; amd §§756, 756-a, 756-b & 757, Gen Bus L",
    "lawSection": "Labor Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5982\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the labor law and the general business law, in\nrelation to the prompt payment of employees and contractors engaged in\nprivate construction projects\n\n\n PURPOSE :\nThis bill amends New York's labor law and prompt pay law to ensure\ngreater enforcement mechanisms are available for employees,\ncontractors and subcontractors.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n§1 - This bill amends Section 196-a of the labor law by adding a new\nsubdivision (ii) which allows an employee, contractor or certified\ncollective bargaining agent to complain to the commissioner of Labor\nregarding a violation or potentially imminent violation of article 6\nof the labor law.\n\n§2 - This bill also amends 756 section (1) of the general business law\nby lowering the aggregate cost threshold for private construction\nprojects from two hundred fifty thousand dollars to one hundred fifty\nthousand dollars; lowering the aggregate size threshold for\nresidential construction projects from 9,000 square feet to 4,500\nsquare feet or less; and lowering the size of applicable residential\npublic housing units from 150 to 75 units.\n\n§3 - This bill amends 756-a section (1) by changing the billing cycle\nto be the calendar month within which the work is performed unless\notherwise stipulated by a construction contract.\n\n§4 - This bill amends 756-b by allowing contractors the use of\narbitration as a permissive remedy for nonpayment.\n\nThe bill amends 756-b by adding a new section (3) that creates a\nsystem of Protocols for the use of arbitration in scenarios of\nnon-payment.\n\n§5 - This bill amends 757 by adding a new section (3) and (4) relating\nto the void provisions of construction contracts and adding new\nlanguage pertaining to the use of arbitration.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nThis bill is necessary to ensure that Payments required by\nconstruction contracts are made in a timely manner.  Failure to pay in\na timely manner often jeopardizes the operations of contractors,\nsubcontractors and material Suppliers and places the wages, benefits\nand jobs of employees at risk.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n2008 Session: A.11520 - John - Reported to Codes Committee/S.3445-C on\nSenate Calendar\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nNone.\n\n LOCAL FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately and applies to construction contracts entered into after\nthe bill becomes law and for contract entered into prior to the\neffective date for which permit or permits have been issued and work\nhas begun.\n",
